.BEACHES...
Issued at 213 PM PST Tue Nov 10 2026

Beach Hazards:

No official Beach Hazard in effect. However, latest buoy reports
still show a dynamic marine environment. Moderate periods will
still make for an energetic surf zone today. Expect another round
of Beach Hazard Statements midweek as longer period swells return.
Hazardous beach conditions are expected to return to the coast later
this week with dangerous conditions forecast for the weekend. A
series of storms within the north Pacific are resulting in a dynamic
fetch scenario, where favorably aligned waves along a storm track
continue to build as the storm advances. This will result in a very
high swell of 20 to 25 feet at 17 to 18 seconds arriving in the
coastal waters late on Friday into Saturday. Surf heights are
expected to top out around 20 to 30 feet, with locally higher waves
at favored locations including Mavericks Beach. A High Surf Warning
this weekend for the Pacific coast is likely. The incoming swell
will also result in a high risk of sneaker waves and rip currents.
Sneaker waves can sweep unaware beachgoers into the water while rip
currents can pull even the strongest swimmers offshore. All these
factors combined will make it a very dangerous day at the beach, and
we strongly advise all people to stay away from the beach during the
weekend.

When visiting the beach, continue to practice beach safety.
Remember, waves can sweep across the shoreline without warning,
pulling people into the sea from beaches, jetties, and rocks. Stay
off of jetties, piers, rocks, and other waterside infrastructure.
Remain out of the water if you`re not an experienced swimmer to
avoid hazardous surf and never turn your back on the ocean!

Coastal Flood Advisory:

A combination of high astronomical tides and thermal expansion from
warmer than normal water will result in tidal flooding concerns
across Bayshore locations in the San Francisco, San Pablo, and
Monterey Bays. At the San Francisco tide gauge, high tide is
expected to be 1.4 ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW) at 11:35 AM
Wednesday 11/11, 1.4 ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW) at 12:10 PM
Thursday 11/12, 1.2 ft above normal (7.0 ft MLLW) at 12:49 PM
Friday 11/13. These predictions include up to 1.5 ft of surge that
will enhance the astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide
varies up to 90 minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast
and through the San Francisco Bay, respectively. In addition,
long-period swell will create hazardous conditions along the
Pacific coast beaches with an increased risk of sneaker waves and
rip currents.

These Spring Tides are due to a new moon on Monday, which is much
lower in the sky this time of year compared to a full moon. In 2
weeks, the astronomical tides will be about 0.8` higher than this
round, and the total water level could be more than a foot higher if
we get SW winds and/or lower pressure. This combination would bring
the total water level near the 1% annual exceedance probability
(2.85` above normal), or a 1 in 100 year event. Something to keep an
eye on for the week of Thanksgiving.

